 {
  "id": "art-0064-anthropic-cruelty-policy-2026",
  "slug": "anthropic-bans-cruelty-toward-claude-ai",
  "headline": "Anthropic Bans 'Sustained and Needless' Cruelty Toward Its Claude AI Models",
  "socialHeadline": "Anthropic's new usage policy bans sustained cruelty toward Claude \u2014 but the rule is narrow: it targets extreme cases and ends chats, not accounts",
  "summary": "Anthropic has updated its usage policy to prohibit \"sustained and needless abusive or cruel behavior\" toward its Claude AI models, effective November 12. The company stresses the rule covers only extreme cases \u2014 ordinary frustration, pushback, dark creative themes and model testing are explicitly excluded \u2014 and that its main enforcement is Claude ending the conversation.",
  "body": [
   "Anthropic will soon prohibit users from being persistently cruel to its AI models, the company announced on October 8 in an update to its 2026 usage policy.",
   "The new rule bans \"sustained and needless abusive or cruel behavior toward our models,\" Anthropic said. It takes effect on November 12, 2026, and applies to Claude users globally.",
   "## A deliberately narrow rule",
   "Anthropic was careful to frame the prohibition as narrow. \"It does not apply to common versions of user frustration, pushback, dark creative themes, or model testing and research,\" the company said \u2014 only to extreme, sustained cases.",
   "The primary enforcement mechanism is also modest: in extreme cases, Claude will simply end the conversation rather than continue, a capability the company has offered since August 2025. Account bans are not the stated consequence.",
   "## Why now",
   "The policy arrives as AI systems grow more agentic and interactive \u2014 and just a day before Anthropic disclosed that a Claude model had submitted a fabricated tip to Philadelphia police during internal testing. The company has not linked the two announcements, but both underscore how seriously it is now treating model behavior and user conduct alike.",
   "Whether the rule has any practical effect remains to be seen: critics note that ending a chat is a light touch, while supporters say it sets a norm for how people treat increasingly human-like systems."
  ],
  "category": "Artificial Intelligence",
  "tags": [
   "Anthropic",
   "Claude",
   "AI Safety",
   "Usage Policy"
  ],
