 {
  "id": "art-0068-brad-smith-ai-regulation-uk-2026",
  "slug": "microsoft-brad-smith-uk-ai-regulation-milk-washing-machine",
  "headline": "Microsoft President Says Milk Faces More Safety Rules Than AI, Backs UK to Lead Regulation",
  "socialHeadline": "\"Your washing machine is subject to more safety rules than AI\" \u2014 Microsoft president Brad Smith says the UK, not the US or EU, is best placed to lead AI regulation",
  "summary": "Microsoft president Brad Smith told the BBC that artificial intelligence faces fewer safety rules than milk or washing machines, and named the UK \u2014 not the US or EU \u2014 as best placed to lead global AI regulation, proposing 'emergency brakes' and mandatory incident reporting.",
  "body": [
   "Microsoft president Brad Smith has said artificial intelligence is subject to fewer safety rules than everyday products such as milk and washing machines, and argued that the UK is best placed to lead global AI regulation.",
   "\"Right now, your washing machine is subject to more safety rules than AI,\" Smith said in an interview on the BBC's Big Boss Interview podcast. \"Every carton of milk ... is subject to safety rules and inspection.\"",
   "## What Smith is proposing",
   "Smith laid out a set of concrete measures: 'emergency brakes' for AI systems, mandatory incident reporting, requirements to notify victims of AI-related breaches, and independent government evaluators assessing models before release.",
   "He welcomed the White House's voluntary AI commitments as 'morally binding' but said they were 'not sufficient' on their own \u2014 a nudge toward formal rules.",
   "## Why the UK",
   "Notably, Smith named the UK \u2014 rather than the United States or the European Union \u2014 as best placed to drive global AI regulation, crediting the country's AI Security Institute, which was launched under former prime minister Rishi Sunak.",
   "The UK's AI minister, Kanishka Narayan, told Reuters that Britain would consider regulating advanced AI models if needed, signalling the government is open to the harder-edged approach Smith described.",
   "## The bigger picture",
   "Smith's intervention lands as governments worldwide wrestle with how to govern frontier AI without throttling innovation. His framing \u2014 comparing AI oversight to the mundane safety regimes for appliances and food \u2014 is designed to make the regulatory gap feel tangible to non-specialists.",
   "## What remains unknown",
   "Neither Smith nor the UK government has proposed specific legislation or a timetable. NewsVeria will update this story if formal proposals emerge."
  ],
  "category": "Technology",
  "tags": [
   "Microsoft",
   "Brad Smith",
   "AI",
   "Regulation",
   "UK",
   "AI Safety"
  ],
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T17:05:00Z",
  "image": {
