 {
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "body": [
   "Japanese industrial automation company Omron has agreed to buy US industrial PC maker OnLogic and its parent company Interlogic for $675 million, the company said Tuesday in a news release. The acquisition is aimed at developing Omron's physical artificial intelligence services within its industrial automation business.",
   "## What the deal includes",
   "Under the agreement, Interlogic will become a wholly owned subsidiary of Omron, while OnLogic will continue to operate as an independent business, retaining its brand, its direct-to-customer model and its current management team, including chief executive Sean Larkin, according to the company's announcement. The transaction was structured as a reverse triangular merger, according to a company-announcement report.",
   "OnLogic develops, manufactures and sells highly configurable, rugged industrial PCs, with more than 90 percent of its revenue coming from the Americas and Europe \u2014 a footprint Omron hopes will broaden its customer base in the United States.",
   "## Why Omron is buying",
   "Omron said it plans to combine OnLogic's rugged industrial computing with its own high-precision control, sensing and robotics technologies to develop next-generation physical AI platforms and manufacturing digital-transformation solutions. The company also expects cross-selling into OnLogic's established customer base to expand its sales.",
   "The move follows a May collaboration agreement between Omron Robotics and Italian automation company Comau to develop automation services for industrial manufacturing, underscoring how quickly Japanese industrial firms are building out AI-centred factory offerings.",
   "## Market context",
   "An earlier Nikkei-sourced report had put the figure at $630 million; the company's official release sets the price at $675 million. Omron's shares were roughly flat in morning trading in Tokyo, compared with a drop of around 2.2 percent for the benchmark Nikkei 225, according to CNA. Completion terms and a closing date were not detailed in the announcement.",
   "NewsVeria will update this story in place if regulators, Omron or OnLogic release further details."
  ],
