  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "body": [
   "Japan's wholesale prices climbed 6.8% in the year to September, data released by the Bank of Japan on Tuesday showed, coming in below the median market forecast of 7.6% and easing from August's 7.6% increase. Companies continued to pass higher fuel and chemical costs through to one another, keeping inflationary pressure in the pipeline elevated.",
   "On a monthly basis, the corporate goods price index fell 0.3%, compared with expectations for a 0.5% rise, following a 0.2% decline in August. The miss offered the central bank only limited comfort: an annual pace near 7% remains far above levels consistent with its price goals, and the details point to cost pressure still working its way through supply chains.",
   "## Import costs surge on weak yen",
   "The clearest sign of pressure came from import costs. The yen-based import price index jumped 24.5% from a year earlier, showing that the currency's weakness keeps pushing up the price of the raw materials Japan buys from abroad. With energy prices high, a soft yen magnifies the effect, raising the domestic cost of fuel and other inputs that businesses then pass on to customers.",
   "## What it means for the BOJ",
   "The figures will be among the inputs the Bank of Japan's board scrutinizes when it produces fresh quarterly growth and inflation forecasts at its policy meeting later this month. Wholesale prices tend to lead consumer inflation, because companies gradually pass higher input costs to households, so the pipeline reading is an important gauge for policymakers judging whether inflation is becoming entrenched.",
   "Markets are watching closely. Analysts had expected September producer inflation to hold near its highest level in more than three years, with the Mace News consensus at 7.6% year on year. The softer 6.8% print still leaves prices rising at a pace that keeps expectations of a near-term rate hike alive."
  ],
