 {
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "body": [
   "The New York Times, Agence France-Presse and The Economist on Monday joined more than 40 publishers in a coalition aimed at preventing artificial intelligence companies from using or profiting from their content without permission, extending the publishing industry's organized pushback against unlicensed AI training.",
   "The Standards for Publisher Usage Rights (SPUR) coalition, launched in February, now has more than 80 members across over 20 countries, according to Reuters. New members also include The Atlantic and Brazil's National Association of Newspapers, which join founding members such as the Financial Times, BBC and Associated Press.",
   "## Standards and tracking systems",
   "SPUR, which is funded entirely by its members, is developing industry-wide standards and technical systems to help publishers track and control how AI companies use their content and to facilitate licensing agreements. The coalition said it recently introduced a Content Telemetry Standard, designed to standardize how AI agents record and report their use of media content so creators can be compensated for their work.",
   "Intellectual property protection and technological advancement can and must co-exist, said Rebecca Grossman-Cohen, the New York Times' head of strategic partnerships and chief of staff, in a statement announcing the move.",
   "## Legal pressure is rising",
   "The coalition's growth comes as publishers step up legal action against AI companies. Earlier this month, USA Today and several newspapers it own